<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/operating-systems-basics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The operating system is the <strong>referee and landlord between programs and
    hardware</strong>: it decides who runs, who gets memory, who touches the disk, and what
    happens when things crash. Linux, Windows and macOS differ in interface but share the same
    four jobs — and each exists because programs cannot be trusted to share nicely on their own.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four jobs of every operating system" widget="equationStep" config={osCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts worth internalising</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>System calls:</strong> the only doorway from a program into the OS (open a file, spawn a process). Everything "the computer does" funnels through a few hundred of them.</li>
    <li><strong>Concurrency hazards:</strong> two processes updating the same bank balance can interleave badly. Locks and atomic operations prevent it — and introduce deadlocks (two programs, each waiting forever on the other). The dining philosophers problem is the classic cartoon of the trap.</li>
    <li><strong>Caches everywhere:</strong> L1/L2/L3 CPU caches, RAM, SSD, disk — a hierarchy where each level is smaller and faster than the next. Programmers who respect locality (touch nearby data together) get free speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Why the kernel matters:</strong> on embedded devices a tiny kernel (RTOS) runs in kilobytes; on servers Linux runs for years without rebooting. Same ideas, wildly different scales.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Tanenbaum, Modern Operating Systems; Arpaci-Dusseau, Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces (free).</p>
</TopicPage>
